<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter6_arrays-of-objects">
    <title>Arrays of Objects</title>

    <p>
        In Chapter 5, we learned that a structure can combine related values
        into one object. For example, a <c>Point</c> structure combines an
        <c>x</c> coordinate and a <c>y</c> coordinate.
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_one_point_before_array" language="cpp">
        <code><![CDATA[
struct Point {
    double x;
    double y;
};

Point point{3.0, 4.0};
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        A structure describes one object. What if a program needs to process
        1,000 points? Creating 1,000 separately named variables would be
        inconvenient and difficult to process with a loop. We need a container
        that can store many objects under one name.
    </p>

    <p>
        The standard-library type <c>std::array</c> provides such a container
        when the number of elements is fixed. The structure defines what one
        element contains; the array organizes many elements.
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_create_std_array"
             language="cpp"
             interactive="yes">
        <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const std::array<int, 5> scores{82, 91, 76, 88, 95};

    for (int score : scores) {
        std::cout << score << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        The type <c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt;</c> means that <c>scores</c> stores
        five <c>int</c> values. Its size is fixed when the array is created. The
        member function <c>size()</c> reports the number of elements.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter6_arrays-of-objects_range-based-loop">
        <title>Range-Based <c>for</c> Loops</title>
        <p>
            The phrase <q>for each score</q> describes a range-based
            <c>for</c> loop. The loop visits each element in order, so a separate
            index is not needed when only the value matters.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter6_arrays-of-objects_indexing">
        <title>Accessing Elements by Index</title>
        <p>
            Each array element has an <term>index</term>, which identifies its
            position. The first element has index 0, not index 1. Indexed
            iteration is useful when a program needs to work with a position or
            compare neighboring elements.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_index_std_array"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const std::array<int, 5> scores{82, 91, 76, 88, 95};

    for (std::size_t index = 0; index < scores.size(); ++index) {
        std::cout << "Score " << index + 1 << ": "
                  << scores[index] << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            Notice the difference between the array index and the displayed
            score number. The index begins at 0, while the human-friendly label
            begins at 1.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter6_arrays-of-objects_search-summarize">
        <title>Searching and Summarizing Values</title>
        <p>
            A loop can inspect every element to search for a value, count values
            that meet a condition, or calculate a total. These tasks combine
            iteration with the counters and accumulators from the previous
            section.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_summarize_scores"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const std::array<int, 5> scores{82, 91, 76, 88, 95};
    int total = 0;
    int high_score_count = 0;

    for (int score : scores) {
        total += score;

        if (score >= 90) {
            ++high_score_count;
        }
    }

    std::cout << "Total: " << total << '\n';
    std::cout << "High scores: " << high_score_count << '\n';
    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter6_arrays-of-objects_index-short-answer">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The first element of a fixed-size array has index
                <fillin width="4" answer="0" mode="number"/>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter6_arrays-of-objects_index-organization">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which loop correctly visits every element of
                <c>std::array&lt;int, 4&gt; values</c> by index?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p><c>for (std::size_t index = 0; index &lt; values.size(); ++index)</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The index begins at 0 and stops before <c>values.size()</c>.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>for (std::size_t index = 0; index &lt;= values.size(); ++index)</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The final valid index is one less than <c>values.size()</c>.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>for (std::size_t index = 1; index &lt; values.size(); ++index)</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This skips the element at index 0.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter6_arrays-of-objects_structures">
        <title>Arrays of Structures</title>

        <p>
            An array can store objects created from a structure. This lets a
            program process several related objects with the same loop pattern.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_array_of_points"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <iostream>

struct Point {
    double x;
    double y;
};

int main() {
    const std::array<Point, 3> points{
        Point{1.0, 2.0},
        Point{3.0, 4.0},
        Point{5.0, 6.0}
    };

    for (const Point& point : points) {
        std::cout << '(' << point.x << ", " << point.y << ")\n";
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The declaration <c>const Point&amp; point</c> gives the loop a
            read-only reference to each object. The loop does not need to copy
            the point, and it cannot modify the array's objects through that
            reference.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter6_arrays-of-objects_classes">
        <title>Arrays of Class Objects</title>

        <p>
            The same idea works with class objects. Instead of accessing public
            data, the loop can call a public member function on each object.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_array_of_rectangles"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <iostream>

class Rectangle {
private:
    double width;
    double height;

public:
    Rectangle(double rectangle_width, double rectangle_height)
        : width{rectangle_width}, height{rectangle_height} {
    }

    double calculate_area() const {
        return width * height;
    }
};

int main() {
    const std::array<Rectangle, 2> rectangles{
        Rectangle{4.0, 3.0},
        Rectangle{5.0, 2.0}
    };

    for (const Rectangle& rectangle : rectangles) {
        std::cout << rectangle.calculate_area() << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The phrase <q>for each object</q> is a useful clue that a
            range-based loop may be appropriate. The type of the object
            determines whether the loop accesses data members or calls member
            functions.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter6_arrays-of-objects_member-function-short-answer">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A function that belongs to a class and is called through an
                object is a <fillin width="20" answer="member function" mode="string"/>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <strcmp use-answer="yes" case="insensitive"/>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter6_arrays-of-objects_class-organization">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which loop organization correctly processes every
                <c>Rectangle</c> object in the array while preserving
                encapsulation?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Use a range-based loop and call the public member
                        function <c>rectangle.calculate_area()</c> for each
                        object.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        The class controls access to its private data through
                        its public operation.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Use a loop that accesses <c>rectangle.width</c> directly
                        from <c>main</c>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        <c>width</c> is private, so outside code should use a
                        public member function.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Create a new <c>Rectangle</c> class inside every loop
                        iteration.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        The array already contains the objects to process.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>
</section>
